## Summary

All four commits do what the prompts specify, on the real code paths, and the branch is safe to merge into `main` while the two live chains run. (1) `tests/test_exp03_record_tools.py` is byte-identical to `165fa36` (and to `4131f7d`, the commit that created it); the CPU-only environment dependence is now handled outside the pinned file by a conftest hook that skips exactly one node id in exactly one file when `torch.cuda.device_count() < 2`, and does nothing otherwise — verified with a real pytest run through a symlinked checkout and with the coder's four-way device-count test. (2) The binder now ties every result sidecar to the loaded approval identity **and** its pins, and every claimed run's four files to the bound completion digests, both ways (subset and completeness): the three review forgeries plus nine variants of mine are refused before any report is written, and the accept direction — a sidecar that also lists the training completion/manifest, the reference manifest, the log and producer sources, exactly as the real producer records — is accepted. Reports are timestamped and exclusive; `check_record.py` verifies the latest; `_safe_json` is back to the reviewed unconditional form. (3) The exp_05 tests build the null template in-test; under filled `approved_digests.json` files for **both** experiments (real closure/checkpoint digests) the nine affected test files are green (454 passed), and the committed templates still parse. (4) Full CPU suite on the clone: **1288 passed, 34 skipped, 0 failed, 0 errors** (the coder's 1202/118 = the same suite minus the 84 tests its no-signal guard excluded, plus the two `test_committed_approval_file_parses` added by `565a56a`). (5) No file of any live closure is in the branch diff; evaluator/writer/frozen-evaluator/training/exp05-evaluator digests are identical at `main`, the tip and the merge commit and equal the live manifests; the launcher closure moves from `efde5a31…` to `9dadbef0…` exactly as round 5 reviewed. **The merge is not conflict-free**: `git merge --no-ff codex-window` on `main` conflicts in one worklog file (`param_efficiency_worklog.md`, both sides appended after the same entry); the resolution is mechanical (keep both, chronological) and touches no code.

Two operational items outside the reviewed code need the Planner's attention (findings 1–2); nothing blocks the merge.

