Author:     Yixun-Hu <yixunhu21@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Fri Sep 18 16:53:55 2026 -0400
Commit:     Yixun-Hu <yixunhu21@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Fri Sep 18 16:53:55 2026 -0400

    exp_05 record: the binding survives a relocated attempt directory
    
    The Planner archives each certified attempt to the NAS and leaves a directory
    symlink where it was.  The binder located `cumulative_hours.json`, the probe
    receipts, the sibling attempts and `final` from the RESOLVED attempt's parent
    and recorded every persisted key -- attempts, runs, products' dependency maps
    -- through `resolve()`, so the move renamed the whole record and `check_record`
    refused evidence that had not changed a byte.  Both halves are red first:
    relocating the attempt alone raises FileNotFoundError on the ledger under the
    destination's parent, and relocating the arm directory (which carries the
    ledger along) then refuses the unchanged `args.json` dependency because the
    recomputed keys are NAS paths while the published sidecars name the originals.
    
    `logical()` is now the identity of everything this report persists or compares:
    the approval's own arm directory and attempt path, each run, and every
    dependency key the products declared.  `stamp` and `snapshot` wrap the
    inherited ones, so the digest checks, the directory listing, the
    resolve-inside-the-directory rule for output entries (a symlink ENTRY is still
    refused) and `revalidate` are untouched -- only the names they hand back
    change.  Where a name cannot be the identity, the filesystem's is: the attempt
    directory is recognised by inode, and `final` must be the same directory rather
    than resolve to the same string.  In the live tree no path has a symlinked
    ancestor, so every key is byte-identical to round 7's report.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

