    exp_05 record: an attempt's name is the one its launcher wrote, not a route to it
    
    The live approval pins every checkpoint through the arm's `final` symlink
    (`ckpt/exp05/S_simple/final/epoch_012.pth`) while the evaluations, the products'
    sidecars and the ledger all name the attempt itself, and the bind is run with
    `--attempt ckpt/exp05/<arm>/final`.  Anchoring the logical identity on the
    approval's own path was therefore wrong for the real record: the fixture pinned
    the attempt directly, so nothing said so.  It now pins through `final` as the
    live approval does, and is red exactly where the live bind would have been --
    `the ledger does not hold this attempt: S_simple`, because the attempt was being
    called `final`.
    
    The published name is the one the launcher wrote into `train_manifest.json` as
    `attempt_path`, which is what every other record spells and what survives both
    `final` and an archiving symlink; the manifest is hash-bound by the completion,
    and it must still name this very directory (inode), which a new forgery checks.
    The approval is honoured by asking the filesystem whether its pinned checkpoint
    and `<attempt>/epoch_012.pth` are one file, so `final` may be either route.
    
    Verified read-only against the real 66 runs, four attempts and five products
    (binder loaded from this worktree with ROOT and --approved pointed at the main
    checkout, as round 7 did): the report is identical to round 7's, field for
    field, apart from `git_HEAD`.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

